// Using AI Assistants at Work

// Objective: Learners will be able to identify tasks that are a good fit for an AI assistant. | Bloom's: understand
::Q1:: Which of these tasks is the best fit for an AI assistant? {
  =Drafting a first version of a team announcement #Drafting is a strength. You review and edit before sending.
  ~Calculating final payroll totals for the month #AI can make math errors. Use your payroll system or a spreadsheet.
  ~Deciding which employee gets a promotion #Decisions about people need human judgment and context the AI doesn't have.
  ~Confirming a vendor's price for today #The AI may give an outdated or invented number. Check the vendor directly.
}

// Objective: Learners will be able to write a prompt that includes a goal, context and format. | Bloom's: apply
::Q2:: Jordan needs to tell a long-time client their order is delayed one week by a supplier. Which prompt will get the most usable first draft? {
  ~"Write an email to our client about their order being delayed by our supplier." #It has a goal and some context, but no format, so the AI has to guess the tone and length.
  ="Write a short apology email to a long-time client about a one-week supplier delay. Warm, under 120 words." #It has a clear goal, the context (who, what, why) and the format (tone and length).
  ~"Write a professional email that is warm, friendly and under 120 words, with a helpful tone." #Format only. The AI doesn't know what happened or who the email is for.
  ~"Our supplier delayed a long-time client's order by one week, and they are a valued customer." #Context only. There's no goal, so the AI doesn't know what you want it to write.
}

// Objective: Learners will be able to decide what information is safe to share with an AI tool. | Bloom's: apply
::Q3:: A coworker wants to paste a customer's name, address and account number into a public AI tool to draft a letter. What should they do instead? {
  =Replace the details with placeholders like [Customer] and fill them in afterward #The AI drafts the letter, and the personal data never leaves your hands.
  ~Paste it in, since most AI tools delete chat history automatically after a while #Many tools store what you paste. Don't assume it gets deleted.
  ~Paste it in, as long as the customer has been with the company for years #How long someone has been a customer doesn't change the data risk.
  ~Share just the account number and leave the customer's name and address out #An account number on its own is still sensitive. Use a placeholder instead.
}

// Objective: Learners will be able to review AI output for errors before using it. | Bloom's: apply
::Q4:: The AI gives you a statistic to put in a report for leadership. What should you do first? {
  =Confirm it in a reliable source before you use it #AI can invent numbers that sound real. Facts always get checked.
  ~Ask the AI to double-check whether the number is right #The AI may repeat the same wrong answer confidently. Check a real source.
  ~Round the number so it looks cleaner in the report #Rounding a made-up number doesn't make it true.
  ~Use it as-is, since AI tools are usually accurate #"Usually" isn't good enough for a report with your name on it.
}

// Objective:  | Bloom's: remember
::Q5:: Which of the following is NOT one of the four prompt habits? {
  ~Goal #Incorrect.
  ~Context #Incorrect.
  =Speed, because a fast prompt always gets the best answer from the assistant #Correct.
  ~All of the above #Incorrect.
}
